# {PROJECT_NAME} — Dataplatform

This dbt project consumes deterministic artifacts produced by the
[ontology hub]({HUB_REPO_URL}).

Operational branching, promotion, rollback, and hotfix guidance is in [`CICD.md`](CICD.md).

## Contract

The hub builds one immutable `CompilePlan` from canonical TTL, source vocabularies, and
closed EntityBindings, then emits generated dbt artifacts. This repository pins an immutable
hub revision or versioned artifact and supplies runtime connections. It never edits
compiler-owned output or reinterprets binding semantics.

## Quick start

```bash
uv sync
# Configure .dbt/profiles.yml from the example without committing credentials.
dbt deps
dbt parse --profiles-dir .dbt
dbt build --profiles-dir .dbt
dbt test --profiles-dir .dbt
```

Use package-qualified `ref()` for hub models. Keep downstream-only logic in ordinary dbt
models. Runtime connection and data-test failures belong here; compile diagnostics belong
in the hub.

## Adapters

Dataplatform setup supports `fabric-lakehouse`, `fabric-warehouse`, and `databricks`.
The corresponding compiler adapters are `fabric` and `databricks`.

## Refreshing a source vocabulary

```bash
dbt run-operation extract_source_schema --args '{source_name: "sample_system"}'
# In the hub:
uv run kairos-ontology import-source --from extracted-schema.yaml --system sample_system
```

Review and redact extracted metadata before committing. Never commit credentials, real
personal data, proprietary sample values, or environment-specific secrets.

## Optional Power BI deployment

The Fabric scaffold includes a workflow that consumes a versioned semantic-model artifact.
Configure repository secrets in GitHub, select an immutable hub tag, and validate TMDL/DAX,
relationships, and security in the deployment toolchain. A compile result does not mean a
release was published or a deployment succeeded.
